Yes—very likely.

The Moon is **28° above the eastern horizon**, far enough up to avoid most horizon obstruction and atmospheric extinction. It is only **8% illuminated** and **32.9° from the Sun**, so it is a thin waning crescent, but the Sun is still about **2.9° below the horizon**—civil twilight. Under a clear sky, the Moon should be readily visible to normal unaided eyesight, especially because its altitude is high.

Look toward the **east**, at an azimuth of about **90°**, roughly one-third of the way from the horizon to overhead. The crescent will likely be fairly faint against the brightening sky, so:

- use an unobstructed view;
- avoid looking near bright lights;
- give your eyes a minute or two to adjust;
- binoculars could help you locate it, though they should not be necessary.

The main caveat is that a very thin crescent can be difficult if the stated illumination is inaccurate or if local haze/clouds are present. Given the values you supplied, however, I would expect it to be visible.

